#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180353} :activity/id #uuid "0e333d7b-9586-5dcc-a2e9-29e8919f1fde" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050442 :topic/id #uuid "49568fcd-874d-513a-8a2c-8089246092fb" :topic/math-academy-id 3674} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180792 :tutorial/content "Recall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis called a logistic growth equation. The positive constant $a > 0$ is the carrying capacity, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below. \n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the general solution to this equation is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\frac{a}{1+C e^{-ak t}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integration constant. \n\nNote the following properties of this general solution:\n\n- Since $a$ and $k$ are both positive, $e^{-akt}\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, $y(t)\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ In other words, $y(t)$ approaches the carrying capacity as $t$ increases. This agrees with the behavior shown in our diagram.\n\n- Since $e^{-akt}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to-\\infty,$ we have that $y(t)\\to 0$ as $t\\to-\\infty.$ This also agrees with our diagram.\n\nWe can use the general solution of the logistic growth equation to make predictions about a population governed by logistic growth. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "7ba42b62-b5a3-5c54-8a92-9ce67945fd36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 981 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3acea89b-a4db-5284-92df-1f9665ae536c" :step/math-academy-id 1924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179532 :question/id #uuid "77bb1db2-e2fb-5126-a116-fce07c309d0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394532}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"} :step/id #uuid "a534edd7-0a0d-55a8-a499-ecfc5a548c59" :step/math-academy-id 27476 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179533 :question/id #uuid "e53b71f8-4ea8-50e7-81c6-62fbd2ac54ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-7408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528009}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "b4f05448-8bec-515f-b01f-973c815d0ffd" :step/math-academy-id 27483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180682 :tutorial/content "The general solution to the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C>0$ is an integration constant. Let's now derive this formula.\n\nOur differential equation is separable, so we start by separating variables and then integrating with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =ky(a-y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =k \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ 1}{y(a-y)} = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{a-y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite the integrand on the left-hand side and complete the integration, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{a}\\int (\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{a-y})\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y-a})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ -\\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =-ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|y-a|-\\operatorname{ln}|y| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|\\frac{y-a}{y}| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{-akt+{C}_{1}} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm C{e}^{-akt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=e^{C_1}$ is a positive constant. \n\nIn our modeling, we have assumed that $0 < y  < a.$ Therefore, the left-hand side of the equation must be negative since the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore, we discard the positive option, and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-a}{y}  =  -C e^{-akt}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-a}{y} & =-C{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y-a & =-Cy{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y+y\\cdot C{e}^{-akt} & =a \\\\ y(1+C{e}^{-akt}) & =a \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba0b74de-5c49-5d2e-a9a6-207220dbb90e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4521 :tutorial/title "Deriving the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "8237a905-9644-5c44-a21f-8bcd1b0638a5" :step/math-academy-id 27816}] :activity/title "Solving the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179831}]}
